What:		/sys/bus/serial/devices/<dev>/pairing_host
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.5
Contact:	linux-input@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RW) Address of the Bluetooth host the attached Joy-Con is
		paired to, as recorded in the controller's own flash, in the
		usual colon-separated form (00:11:22:33:44:55).

		A Joy-Con is the same controller on the rail and over
		Bluetooth, and it keeps the host it belongs to. Reading this
		says which adapter it would reconnect to once detached.

		Reading returns -ENODATA for a controller that has never been
		paired, and until the record has been read from the
		controller: a moment after it is docked, after system sleep
		until the controller on the rail has been found to be the
		same one, and from the start of a pairing written here until
		its outcome has been read back.

		Writing an address pairs the controller to that host over the
		rail, with no SYNC button and no Bluetooth pairing, and
		replaces the record that was there - a controller holds one
		host. The write blocks until the controller has stored it and
		the record read back from it names that host, at most three
		seconds, and returns:

		==========  ==================================================
		-EINVAL     the address does not parse
		-ENODEV     the rail is empty, or the controller left or was
		            swapped while pairing
		-EBUSY      another pairing write is in flight
		-EIO        the controller refused a step or answered one
		            out of sequence, or the record read back names
		            another host
		-ETIMEDOUT  the controller stopped answering; the first
		            pairing of a controller fresh from the factory
		            writes its flash first and can take this long,
		            so retry once
		==========  ==================================================

		or the error of the serial write. A writer killed while
		waiting stops waiting; the pairing is abandoned as if it had
		failed.

		A failed or abandoned write leaves the controller with the
		record it had or the new one. The driver then opens the rail
		session again, so that no answer to the abandoned pairing can
		be taken for one to the next, and reads the record again:
		reading the attribute returns -ENODATA until it has, then the
		record the controller holds. System sleep waits for a write
		in flight to finish.

What:		/sys/bus/serial/devices/<dev>/pairing_record
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.5
Contact:	linux-input@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RO) The pairing pairing_host describes, with its Bluetooth
		link key: the host address as pairing_host prints it, a space,
		and the key as 32 hex digits, all from one record.

		A detached Joy-Con reconnects by paging the host it stored and
		asking it for this key. Only the controller has it - it is not
		established through bonding, so a Bluetooth host has no way to
		learn it - and a host that cannot answer has to pair again,
		which is what a user notices as a controller that never comes
		back on its own. Reading it here lets that host be told.

		Readable by root only. Returns -ENODATA under the same
		conditions as pairing_host.
